,0.0,0.0,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,-4.768370445162873e-07,-2.3841855067985307e-07,0.0,-0.6851255893707275,0.0,-3.4570634852570947e-06,0.0,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,0.0,-6.198863957251888e-06,-0.005494136828929186,0.0,0.0,0.0,-0.012784544378519058,-1.7881377516459906e-06,0.0,-2.861018856492592e-06,-3.099436753473128e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,50,70206,57960,9407,220,16,15,24884,43298,1124,9407,220,18,15,24884,43298,1124,9407,220,20,15,24884,43298,1124,9407,220,22,15,24884,43298,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nSimplify $\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"2af3602d76c3376f3ee7bcf9a054d2c1d4481e4955dede0878b3c472bbe40c36"},"seconds":57.967530488967896,"seed":69079814700323342,"stop":"eos","text":"To simplify the expression \\(\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ\\), we can use trigonometric identities and properties of angles. Let's proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, recall that \\(\\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). We can substitute this in:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ = \\cos 10^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\cos 50^\\circ \\cdot \\cos 70^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the identity \\(\\cos x = \\sin (90^\\circ - x)\\). Therefore, \\(\\cos 70^\\circ = \\sin 20^\\circ\\) and \\(\\cos 50^\\circ = \\sin 40^\\circ\\). Substituting these in, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nNow, we use the product-to-sum identities and properties of the product of sines. We know that:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (20^\\circ - 40^\\circ) - \\cos (20^\\circ + 40^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (-20^\\circ) - \\cos 60^\\circ] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2}] = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4}.\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4} \\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ \\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nWe can further simplify \\(\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ\\) using the product-to-sum identities:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (10^\\circ - 20^\\circ) + \\cos (10^\\circ + 20^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (-10^\\circ) + \\cos 30^\\circ] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 10^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}] = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting back, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\right) - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ + \\frac{3}{16} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ = \\frac{3}{16}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified value of \\(\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{16}}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{3}{16}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
